    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/adgeo-35-79-2013",
    "abstract": "Abstract. An Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) approach was used to reproduce the precipitation anomalies for the rainy seasons over the south and north parts of the Northeast of Brazil (NEB) during 1982\u20132009 period. The seasonal hindcasts of precipitation anomalies from Climate Forecast System v2 (CFSv2) model and the observed dominant modes of anomalous Sea Surface Temperature over the South and North Atlantic Ocean were used as explanatory variables separately. The reduction of dispersion between the explanatory and dependent variables after the fit of the networks suggest the ANN as an important complementary technique for the climate studies over the NEB. However, a large dataset are required to the models capture the non-linear process in more details. The practical implication of the results is that ANNs constructed here could be applied in further analyses, for example, to explore the ANN's ability in improving the seasonal climate forecasts considering that the numerical and statistical methods must be complementary tools.",
    "field": "Statistical climate downscaling",
    "problem": "General Circulation Models (GCMs) have serious difficulties modeling seasonal precipitation regimes in Northeast Brazil, partly due to poor spatial resolution, which this study addresses by exploring statistical downscaling using Artificial Neural Networks to improve forecast accuracy.",
    "method": "This paper uses an Artificial Neural Network approach to reproduce seasonal precipitation anomalies over Northeast Brazil by separately using either CFSv2 model hindcasts or observed Sea Surface Temperature modes as explanatory variables for statistical downscaling.",
    "task": "The task is to compare CFSv2 model hindcasts against an Artificial Neural Network-based statistical downscaling method for reproducing seasonal precipitation anomalies over the Northeast of Brazil.",
    "model": [
        "CFSv2",
        "CFSv1",
        "CAM3"
    ],
